# A to Z Technical

The exact Home Assistant words to use. For general writing, see [A to Z General](a-to-z.html).

<%
  register = JSON.parse(File.read(File.join(root, 'data', 'ha-terminology.json')))
  sources = register['sources'].to_h { |source| [source['id'], source] }
  kinds = {
    'official-glossary' => 'Home Assistant term', 'entity-platform' => 'Entity platform',
    'automation-language' => 'Automations and scripts', 'state-records' => 'State record', 'dashboard-language' => 'Dashboard',
    'energy-language' => 'Energy', 'security-language' => 'Accounts and security', 'helper-types' => 'Helper type',
    'assist-language' => 'Assist and voice', 'installation-language' => 'Installation', 'additional-entity-domains' => 'Entity domain',
    'view-types' => 'Dashboard view type', 'configuration-keys' => 'Configuration key'
  }
  # Four categories repeat one rule for many names. They are grouped into one entry each, with a row per name.
  groups = {
    'card-types' => { name: 'card types', id: 'card-types', lead: 'The card names the card editor shows. Use the name as shown when you point to a card.', column: 'What it shows' },
    'card-features' => { name: 'card features', id: 'card-features', lead: 'The features the card editor can add to a card. Use the name as shown when you point to a feature.', column: 'Use it to' },
    'selector-types' => { name: 'selector types', id: 'selector-types', lead: 'The kinds of input a configuration field accepts. Ask the question in plain words and keep the selector name as written in configuration.', column: 'Accepts' },
    'configuration-keys' => { name: 'configuration keys and values', id: 'configuration-keys', lead: 'The keys and values written in dashboard configuration. Write each one exactly as shown, in code. In a sentence, use the plain wording given.', column: 'What it does' },
    'settings-labels' => { name: 'settings destinations', id: 'settings-destinations', lead: 'The destinations in Settings. Write each label exactly as Settings shows it.', column: 'Use it to' }
  }
  singles = register['terms'].reject { |term| groups.key?(term['category']) }.map { |term| { name: term['native_name'], term: term } }
  grouped = groups.map { |category, group| { name: group[:name], group: group, terms: register['terms'].select { |term| term['category'] == category } } }
  entries = (singles + grouped).sort_by { |entry| [entry[:name].downcase, entry[:term] ? entry[:term]['id'] : entry[:group][:id]] }
  by_letter = entries.group_by { |entry| entry[:name][0].upcase }
  letters = ('A'..'Z').to_a
  fmt = lambda { |text| escape(text).gsub(/`([^`]+)`/, '<code>\1</code>') }
%>
<nav role="navigation" aria-label="A to Z navigation">
<ol class="style-a-z" role="list"><% letters.each do |letter| %><% if by_letter[letter] %><li><a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="#<%= letter.downcase %>"><%= letter %></a></li><% else %><li><span class="style-a-z__empty"><%= letter %></span></li><% end %><% end %></ol>
</nav>

<% by_letter.keys.sort.each do |letter| %>
<h2 class="style-a-z__letter" id="<%= letter.downcase %>"><%= letter %></h2>
<% by_letter[letter].each do |entry| %>
<% if entry[:group] %>
<% group = entry[:group] %>
<div class="lexicon-entry" id="<%= group[:id] %>">
<h3><%= group[:name] %></h3>
<p><strong class="civica-tag civica-tag--grey">Home Assistant term</strong></p>
<p><%= fmt.call(group[:lead]) %></p>
<table><caption><%= group[:name].capitalize %></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col"><%= group[:column] %></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<% entry[:terms].sort_by { |term| term['native_name'].downcase }.each do |term| %>
<tr id="<%= escape(term['id'].downcase) %>"><th scope="row"><%= escape(term['native_name']) %><% if term['status'] == 'historical-term-retained' %><br><small>Older name, kept for saved settings</small><% end %></th><td><%= fmt.call(term['first_use_explanation'].sub(/\AAccepts /, '').sub(/\A([a-z])/) { $1.upcase }) %></td></tr>
<% end %>
</tbody></table>
</div>
<% else %>
<% term = entry[:term] %>
<div class="lexicon-entry" id="<%= escape(term['id'].downcase) %>">
<h3><%= escape(term['native_name']) %><% if term['code_identifiers'] %> <small>(<code><%= escape(term['code_identifiers'].join(', ')) %></code>)</small><% end %></h3>
<p class="govuk-hint"><strong class="civica-tag civica-tag--grey">Home Assistant term</strong> <%= kinds.fetch(term['category'], term['category']) %><%= ' · older name, kept for saved settings' if term['status'] == 'historical-term-retained' %></p>
<p><%= fmt.call([term['first_use_explanation'], term['avoid_confusion'], term['household_wording']].join(' ')) %></p>
<% unless term['aliases'].nil? || term['aliases'].empty? %><p>Also called: <%= escape(term['aliases'].join(', ')) %>.</p><% end %>
<% if term['official_glossary_url'] %><p><a href="<%= escape(term['official_glossary_url']) %>">Home Assistant glossary entry</a></p><% end %>
<% if term['category'] == 'entity-platform' %>
<% values = register['native_values'].select { |value| value['domain'] == term['code_identifiers'].first } %>
<% unless values.empty? %>
<details><summary><%= values.length %> context-specific native labels for <%= escape(term['code_identifiers'].first) %></summary>
<table><caption>Labels, keys and what each one is</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Label and context</th><th scope="col">Key and kind</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<% values.each do |value| %>
<tr id="<%= escape(value['id'].downcase) %>"><th scope="row"><%= escape(value['native_label']) %><br><small><%= escape(value['context']) %></small></th><td><code><%= escape(value['code']) %></code><br><%= escape(value['code_kind'].tr('-', ' ')) %></td><td><%= escape(value['first_use_explanation']) %><br><a href="<%= escape(sources.fetch(value['source_id'])['url']) %>">Translation file</a> · <code><%= escape(value['native_label_pointer']) %></code></td></tr>
<% end %>
</tbody></table>
</details>
<% end %>
<% end %>
</div>
<% end %>
<% end %>
<% end %>

## Kinds of value

| Kind | How to use it |
| --- | --- |
| Entity ID | A full ID. Use the real ID from the household’s setup |
| Domain | A family of entities. Not a full entity ID |
| Raw state | Read it with its domain and device class |
| Attribute property | A key inside the state. Not an entity ID |
| Attribute value | The value of one attribute. Not the same as a selected mode |
| Device-class key | Names the label for that class in that domain |
| Default translation key | A default entry in a translation file (`_`). Do not copy it anywhere |

## Other entity domains

Home Assistant’s screens refer to 15 more domains than its 45 registered platforms. This table maps each one, plus Plant, Zone and Schedule.

<% terms_by_id = register['terms'].to_h { |term| [term['id'], term] } %>
<table><caption>Supplementary entity-domain mapping</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Exact domain</th><th scope="col">Entry</th><th scope="col">Source scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<% register['supplemental_domain_mappings'].sort_by { |mapping| mapping['domain'] }.each do |mapping| %>
<% term = terms_by_id.fetch(mapping['entry_id']) %>
<tr><th scope="row"><code><%= escape(mapping['domain']) %></code></th><td><a href="#<%= escape(term['id'].downcase) %>"><%= escape(term['native_name']) %></a></td><td><%= escape(mapping['source_role']) %> · <a href="<%= escape(mapping['official_guidance_url']) %>">Official <%= escape(mapping['domain']) %> guidance</a></td></tr>
<% end %>
</tbody></table>

## About this lexicon

It was checked against Home Assistant Core 2026.11.1, frontend 20261028.5 and the official website on 12 November 2026. That is the version we checked, not the version in a reader’s home. It has <%= register['terms'].length %> entries and <%= register['native_values'].length %> native labels. They are different things, so do not add them together.

English labels are here so you can find an entry. Keep the translation Home Assistant shows, and the names people gave their devices. The underscore `_` in a native-label table is a default translation entry, not an entity ID.

If a term is missing, add it from a source. Do not guess. The [terminology JSON](../../reference/ha-terminology.json) holds every source, ID and date.

The official Home Assistant website identifies its source under [CC BY-NC-SA 4.0](https://github.com/home-assistant/home-assistant.io/blob/644bcefb1b42d1f50d26847d7d11df06c3b84ae2/LICENSE.md). This lexicon keeps its term names, aliases and source links and links to the official definitions instead of copying them. The household explanations and examples were written for Civica.

The native keys and English labels come from Home Assistant Core and frontend contributors under [Apache License 2.0](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/LICENSE.md). They keep their upstream licence. They are not Civica translations.
